[
  {
    "n": 1,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "(This feature was released in v1.1.0)",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 2,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "JSON Schema is a draft standard for describing the format of JSON data. The schema itself is also JSON data. By validating a JSON structure with JSON Schema, your code can safely access the DOM without manually checking types, or whether a key exists, etc. It can also ensure that the serialized JSON conform to a specified schema.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 3,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "RapidJSON implemented a JSON Schema validator for {1}. If you are not familiar with JSON Schema, you may refer to {2}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"http://json-schema.org/documentation.html\">JSON Schema Draft v4</a>",
      "<a href=\"http://spacetelescope.github.io/understanding-json-schema/\">Understanding JSON Schema</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 4,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Basic Usage",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 5,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "First of all, you need to parse a JSON Schema into {1}, and then compile the {2} into a {3}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">Document</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Document</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaDocument</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 6,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Secondly, construct a {1} with the {2}. It is similar to a {3} in the sense of handling SAX events. So, you can use {4} to validate a document, and then check the validity.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaValidator</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaDocument</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Writer</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">document.Accept(validator)</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 7,
    "tag": "div",
    "text": "String buffer with UTF8 encoding.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 8,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Some notes:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 9,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "One {1} can be referenced by multiple {2}s. It will not be modified by {3}s.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaDocment</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaValidator</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaValidator</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 10,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "A {1} may be reused to validate multiple documents. To run it for other documents, call {2} first.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaValidator</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">validator.Reset()</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 11,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Validation during parsing/serialization",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 12,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Unlike most JSON Schema validator implementations, RapidJSON provides a SAX-based schema validator. Therefore, you can parse a JSON from a stream while validating it on the fly. If the validator encounters a JSON value that invalidates the supplied schema, the parsing will be terminated immediately. This design is especially useful for parsing large JSON files.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 13,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "DOM parsing",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 14,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "For using DOM in parsing, {1} needs some preparation and finalizing tasks, in addition to receiving SAX events, thus it needs some work to route the reader, validator and the document. {2} is a helper class that doing such work.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">Document</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaValidatingReader</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 15,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "SAX parsing",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 16,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "For using SAX in parsing, it is much simpler. If it only need to validate the JSON without further processing, it is simply:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 17,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This is exactly the method used in the {1} example. The distinct advantage is low memory usage, no matter how big the JSON was (the memory usage depends on the complexity of the schema).",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/Tencent/rapidjson/blob/f54b0e47a08782a6131cc3d60f94d038fa6e0a51/example/schemavalidator/schemavalidator.cpp\">schemavalidator</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 18,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "If you need to handle the SAX events further, then you need to use the template class {1} to set the output handler of the validator:",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">GenericSchemaValidator</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 19,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Serialization",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 20,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "It is also possible to do validation during serializing. This can ensure the result JSON is valid according to the JSON schema.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 21,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Of course, if your application only needs SAX-style serialization, it can simply send SAX events to {1} instead of {2}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaValidator</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Writer</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 22,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Remote Schema",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 23,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "JSON Schema supports {1}, which is a {2} referencing to a local or remote schema. Local pointer is prefixed with {3}, while remote pointer is an relative or absolute URI. For example:",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"http://spacetelescope.github.io/understanding-json-schema/structuring.html\"><span class=\"tt\">$ref</span> keyword</a>",
      "<a class=\"el\" href=\"/docs/rapidjson/v1-1-0/en/01-guide/08-pointer/\" title=\"Pointer\">JSON pointer</a>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">#</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 24,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "As {1} does not know how to resolve such URI, it needs a user-provided {2} instance to do so.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SchemaDocument</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">IRemoteSchemaDocumentProvider</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 25,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Conformance",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 26,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "RapidJSON passed 262 out of 263 tests in {1} (Json Schema draft 4).",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/json-schema/JSON-Schema-Test-Suite\">JSON Schema Test Suite</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 27,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The failed test is \"changed scope ref invalid\" of \"change resolution scope\" in {1}. It is due to that {2} schema keyword and URI combining function are not implemented.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">refRemote.json</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">id</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 28,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Besides, the {1} schema keyword for string values is ignored, since it is not required by the specification.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">format</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 29,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Regular Expression",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 30,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The schema keyword {1} and {2} uses regular expression to match the required pattern.",
    "tokens": [
